{"author":{"affiliation_history":[{"country_code":"US","id":"I148283060","name":"Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory","years":[2020,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026]},{"country_code":"US","id":"I103922791","name":"QB3","years":[2025]},{"country_code":"US","id":"I95457486","name":"University of California, Berkeley","years":[2025]},{"country_code":"US","id":"I137902535","name":"North Carolina State University","years":[2019,2020,2021]},{"country_code":"US","id":"I201448701","name":"University of Washington","years":[2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021]},{"country_code":"CN","id":"I4210094772","name":"Henan University of Engineering","years":[2019]},{"country_code":"CN","id":"I134687103","name":"Nanjing Tech University","years":[2010,2011]},{"country_code":"CN","id":"I4391768075","name":"State Key Laboratory of Materials-Oriented Chemical Engineering","years":[2010,2011]}],"author_id":"A5019229507","ca_plastic_works_count":4,"ca_works_count":4,"citations_by_year":[{"all":52,"plastic":0,"year":2017},{"all":59,"plastic":0,"year":2018},{"all":68,"plastic":0,"year":2019},{"all":60,"plastic":1,"year":2020},{"all":104,"plastic":8,"year":2021},{"all":138,"plastic":13,"year":2022},{"all":123,"plastic":14,"year":2023},{"all":150,"plastic":32,"year":2024},{"all":169,"plastic":40,"year":2025},{"all":199,"plastic":41,"year":2026}],"cited_by_count":1259,"current_in_ca":false,"domain_tags":[],"early_career":false,"first_pub_year":2010,"first_year":2020,"h_index":15,"institution_ids":["I103922791","I137902535","I148283060","I95457486"],"last_ca_year":2025,"last_year":2025,"m_quotient":0.94,"name":"Chang Dou","orcid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9871-2641","plastic_basis":"openalex","plastic_citations":149,"plastic_works_count":6,"primary_affiliations":[{"country_code":"US","id":"I148283060","name":"Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory","source":"openalex_last_known"}],"rising_pct":72,"rising_score":0.0061,"work_count":4,"work_ids":["W3102371844","W4388349917","W4388759090","W4408174811"]},"schema_version":"s3.1","works":[{"abstract":"Society\u2019s wish list for future packaging systems is placing some daunting challenges upon researchers: In addition to protecting contents during storage and shipping, the material must not bio-accumulate, and it should be readily recyclable by using practical processing steps. This article considers strategies employing bio-based plastics and reviews published information relative to their performance. Though bioplastics such as poly(lactic acid) (PLA) and poly(hydroxybutyrate) (PHB) can be prepared from plant materials, their default properties are generally inferior to those of popular synthetic plastics. In addition, some bioplastics are not easily decomposed in soil or seawater, and the polymers can undergo chemical breakdown during recycling. This review considers strategies to overcome such challenges, including the use of biodegradable cellulose-based reinforcing particles. In addition to contributing to strength, the cellulose can swell the bioplastic, allowing enzymatic attack. The rate-controlling step in bioplastic degradation also can be abiotic, i.e. not involving enzymes. Though there is much more work to be done, much progress has been achieved in formulating bioplastic composites that are biodegradable, recyclable, and higher in strength compared to the neat polymer. Emphasis in this review is placed on PLA and PHB, but not to the exclusion of other bioplastic matrix materials.","author_count":4,"author_ids":["A5019229507","A5021322218","A5066771968","A5083527126"],"author_institution_ids":["I137902535","I148283060"],"authors":[{"id":"A5021322218","name":"Martin Allen Hubbe"},{"id":"A5083527126","name":"Nathalie Lavoine"},{"id":"A5066771968","name":"Lucian A. Lucia"},{"id":"A5019229507","name":"Chang Dou"}],"ca_any_author":true,"ca_led":true,"cited_by_count":73,"doi":"https://doi.org/10.15376/biores.16.1.hubbe","domain_tags":["fate_transport_and_sorption","soil_and_agriculture"],"facet_tags":["matrix:food_and_drink","matrix:soil_and_agriculture"],"institution_ids":["I137902535","I148283060"],"is_oa":true,"journal":"BioResources","last_updated":"2026-11-10T11:39:43.021295","license":"","needs_review":false,"oa_status":"gold","oa_url":"https://bioresources.cnr.ncsu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/BioRes_16_1_2021_Hubbe_LLD_REVIEW_Fomulating_Bioplastic_Composites_Recyc_Performce_18024.pdf","openalex_topics":["T10488","T10513","T10661"],"plastic_related":true,"plastic_tier":"core","primary_topic":"T10661","pub_date":"2020-11-01","study_type":"article","title":"Formulating bioplastic composites for biodegradability, recycling, and performance: A Review","type":"article","work_id":"W3102371844","year":2020},{"abstract":"Limited biotic and abiotic technologies to process increasing deposits of synthetic polymers (such as polyolefins, polyesters, polyamides) and recalcitrant natural polymers (for instance, lignin predominantly from pulp and paper industries) remains a topic of discussion across the globe.In this regard, ionic liquids (ILs), also commonly described as \"designer solvents\" have attracted much attention for recycling and upcycling of these polymers.Based on the ease of structural modifications to tune the physicochemical properties of ILs, we designed and studied ILs to generate nontechnical lignin from biomass with unique properties as discussed in this presentation.Furthermore, we also demonstrate the ILcatalyzed depolymerization of the generated lignin into bio-accessible intermediates to aid bioconversion.Lastly, we discuss an integrated one-pot IL-based hydrolysis of mixture of polyesters -polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and poly lactic acid (PLA) -followed by bioconversion of the depolymerized stream using P. putida.","author_count":8,"author_ids":["A5000312223","A5019229507","A5046743032","A5059679554","A5073802212","A5090913410","A5100384109","A5100406415"],"author_institution_ids":[],"authors":[{"id":"A5046743032","name":"Hemant Choudhary"},{"id":"A